<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mandate of Heaven</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tianming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mandate of Heaven, tianming in Chinese, is the doctrine that Heaven grants the right to rule to a virtuous ruler and withdraws it from a wicked or incompetent one. It was formulated by the early Zhou to justify their overthrow of the Shang dynasty around 1046 BCE. Later Chinese thinkers and dynasties used it to explain the rise and fall of ruling houses, treating rebellion, disasters and defeat as signs that the mandate had been lost.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven.jpg" alt="Mandate of Heaven" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peenwald · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dynastic_Cycle.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the doctrine, Heaven (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a>) bestows its mandate on a virtuous ruler, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven">Son of Heaven</a> (Tianzi), who rules all under heaven (Tianxia). It does not require noble birth: the Han and Ming dynasties were founded by men of common origins, who were seen as successful because they had gained the mandate. Keeping it depended on the just and able performance of the ruler and his heirs.</p><p>The Zhou were the first to use the concept to explain their right to rule. They held that the Shang came to power because the Xia had lost their mandate, and that the Shang ruling house had become morally corrupt. Early records such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Da Yu</a> ding inscription speak of 'the great command in the sky'. Heaven's authority was held to pass through the founder, King Wen, to his heirs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Eastern Zhou the kings lost real power but not their divine legitimacy, while inscriptions show that the rulers of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> held privately that their ancestors had received the mandate. Qin's own surviving material never mentions it. Han writers painted the Qin as tyrannical and so as having lost the mandate, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, who came from outside the aristocracy, was credited with a magical birth and later a divine ancestry.</p><p>In 220 CE the last Han emperor abdicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a>, and the court prognosticator Xu Zhi listed signs showing that Wei should succeed the Han. To justify their own succession the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> had the scholar Xue Juzheng argue that the mandate had passed through each of the Five Dynasties. The Qing, a Manchu dynasty, argued that peasant rebels led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> had overthrown the Ming and that they themselves had won the mandate by defeating those rebels and restoring stability.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> held that the people are of supreme importance and the ruler last, so the mandate gave no unconditional right to rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Feizi</a> rejected the idea of a just rebellion entirely. The right of rebellion was never written into law, and rebellion was always punished. Because the winner decided who held the mandate, some Chinese scholars regard it as victor's justice, and accounts of a dynasty's fall must be read with caution.</p><p>The idea spread to other East Asian countries. The Joseon dynasty made it an enduring state ideology, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> it gave the emperor the right to rule on his competence rather than his lineage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiho-code">Taihō Code</a> of 703 adapted Tang government but omitted it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mandate of Heaven” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zhou conquerors absorbed Shang scribes, craftsmen and ritual specialists and justified their own rule by contrasting the virtue of their founders with the alleged depravity of the last Shang king, the origin of the theory of the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The doctrine of the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b> (tianming), set out in chapters of the Book of Documents attributed to the Duke of Zhou, held that Heaven entrusted rule to the virtuous and withdrew it from the wicked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…physical heavens and the home of gods and ancestors, and the source of moral meaning, as in the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b>; qi, the breath or energy of which all things are made, gods included; the veneration of ancestors; and bao ying, moral reciprocity, by which every being is…</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Shulgi of Ur declared himself divine in the 21st century BCE, the pharaohs of Egypt were regarded as gods on earth, Chinese emperors ruled by the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b>, and the Sapa Inca was revered as the son of the sun god Inti.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Purpose was the hardest to judge, since almost every ruler claimed to serve the common good; in China the doctrine of the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b> left the judgement to Heaven, which withdrew the right to rule from a wicked or incompetent ruler.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b> the right to rule rested on virtue, not birth: the Ming founder, the Hongwu Emperor, had been a penniless peasant and Buddhist monk.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
